/* SEQ 7 — KARNAK — pages 51–63 */

EXT. NILE, NORTH OF LUXOR - NIGHT

SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 21:00.

Starlight only. The police launch runs without lights. Both banks black. No streetlamps, no lit minarets. A whole city with its power off.

In the stern, TUT sits facing west, hood up, as he has since noon. Under his jacket a pale-green glow pulses. Stutters. Pulses. His right hand trembles on the ebony stick. He stills it with his left.

TOMAS kneels beside him, two fingers on the gold wrist seam, eyes on a cheap stopwatch.

TOMAS
The core's losing regulation. The stalls will come closer together.

TUT
Then I will walk in the gaps.

At the bow, NOUR lowers the binoculars. On the east bank, the only light for miles: a white glow over KARNAK. A slow wink of flashes.

NOUR
That's the Ninth Pylon. It's taking it down. Horemheb packed Akhenaten's temples inside it as fill. Talatat. The French took thousands out of the west wing, starting in the sixties.
(beat)
That's the east wing.

RAMI, splinted left hand, yellow windbreaker, flips his notebook open.

RAMI
Forty-four. "Why did Horemheb hide the talatat instead of smashing them?"

TUT
Stone is expensive. Hatred is cheap. You fill a pylon with what you have.

NOUR
And by burying them he saved them. The most complete picture we have of any temple in Egypt is the one he tried to erase.

Rami scribbles, delighted.

ADAEZE
The glass said "whoever would ascend must first be weighed." Nothing about how. If it's digging, it's missing a rule.

TUT
Then that is the first thing it has wanted that it does not have.

At the wheel, TAREK watches the glow.

TAREK
Then we take it first.

EXT. KARNAK, AVENUE OF RAMS - NIGHT

The launch noses into a dark landing below the Corniche. PVT. MINA and Tomas stay aboard.

Up the steps: Tarek, FATHI, PVT. YOUSSEF, PVT. KARIM, Nour, ADAEZE, Rami with the conservation-kit bag, Tut on his stick. Ram-headed sphinxes by starlight. The First Pylon. Far inside, the work light.

Overhead, white pinpoints drift across the stars. FLIES, trailing hair-thin glints.

Fathi stops behind a ram. Palms up, lips moving, a few private seconds. He finds Tut watching.

FATHI
For all of us, ya Malik. It costs the same.

INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL - NIGHT

A forest of stone. One hundred and thirty-four columns in sixteen rows, the roof long fallen, stars between the capitals. Down the centre, twelve great columns climb twenty-one metres into the dark.

Flies cling to the shafts like moths on a porch.

Over the central nave hangs a SEARCHLIGHT DRONE, turning slowly. Its beam rakes the forest —

— and for one heartbeat the hall is strung with silver. THREADS, hundreds, column to column. Shin. Waist. Throat.

A loom.

The beam moves on. Black.

FATHI
(whisper)
Every thread ends at a fly. Touch one, they all know.

Down the nave, three thin RED LINES pace between the great columns. JACKALS: matte black, reverse-jointed, a rifle module along each spine.

FATHI (CONT'D)
The axis is theirs. We take the aisles. Eight seconds a pass.

Silver. They memorise. Black. They move.

Fathi guides Nour's boot over a shin-thread by the ankle. Silver: everyone flat to a column. Black: Rami ducks a throat-thread, splint to his chest.

Tut steps. The black ceramic foot lifts —

— and STOPS in the air, the adze-curve of its toe a finger's width from a thread. It will not come down. He sways on the stick.

Behind him, the searchlight begins its turn.

Fathi takes Tut's weight under the arms and holds him still. A statue among statues.

Silver. A red line in the nave swings toward the aisle. Pauses.

Black. The foot unlocks. Tut sets it down beyond the thread.

TUT
(barely)
Six seconds.

FATHI
I counted seven, ya Malik.

INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL, THIRD PYLON - CONTINUOUS

The east edge of the forest, in the searchlight's spill. A wall of broken stone. Tut lays his palm on it.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I put it here. My stela. Red granite, taller than a man.

NOUR
(in English)
Legrain found it in pieces, in the north-east corner. Nineteen-oh-five.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
They built a forest of columns over it and wrote Horemheb's name on my words.
(beat)
This I know from it. He did not even need a new name. He kept the middle of mine.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Nebkheperure. Djeserkheperure. He changed "lord" to "holy" and kept the rest.
[[verify: nb → ḏsr recut attested at Luxor Temple (res. 09 §3.3); confirm it on this stela (res. 01 §19)]]

Tut looks back into the dark hall.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I wrote here that the gods had stopped answering. I meant it as good news.

EXT. KARNAK, COURT OF THE NINTH PYLON - NIGHT

SUPER: 00:31.

White floodlight. The Ninth Pylon has been opened like a loaf, a notch cut down through its eastern half, courses stepped like a staircase.

Down the steps stands a line of SHABTI, bone-white, faceless, one amber slit each. A BUCKET-CHAIN. Block to hands, hands to hands. Each hand-off, a faint ceramic TICK. The rhythm never changes.

At the top of the chain stands THE REIS: taller than the rest, a black band across its slit, a jackal-profile mast bolted to its head.

At the foot, shabti carry the blocks one at a time into the dark court and lay each face up on a square of white foam. ROWS of them. Empty steel BLOCK TROLLEYS wait at the row ends.

Behind a fallen architrave, Adaeze lies prone beside Nour, the others behind them.

ADAEZE
It's laying them on conservation foam. It's ending the world and it's worried about chipping.

Above the rows, a CAMERA DRONE glides low and FLASHES. One row on. FLASH. One row. FLASH. Toward the chain. Toward them.

A block comes down the chain into the flood. Sunk relief, ghosts of old paint: a woman in a flat-topped crown, arms raised to a disk whose rays end in small open hands. Across the offering table, facing her: the same woman.

Something leaves Tut's face.

TUT
That is the queen's temple.

NOUR
Hwt-benben. The queen makes the offering alone. The king never appears.

TUT
He was elsewhere. He was usually elsewhere.

ADAEZE
It's photographed every face out of that pylon, and it's still digging.

TUT
Then it is not on a face.
(beat)
Turn it over.

EXT. KARNAK, BLOCK FIELD - CONTINUOUS

The newest rows, in the dark between the flood and the sweep.

They crawl in. Fathi and Karim tip a block on edge without a sound. Nour's fingertips run the underside. Old mortar. Nothing. Down. The next.

Behind them: FLASH. Adaeze holds up four fingers. Rows left.

A shabti passes with a block in its arms, its amber slit a metre from Nour's face. Lays it down. Turns. Walks back. Tick. Tick.

FLASH. Three fingers.

The next block. Under the mortar, Nour's fingers find smooth stone. Then lines. Cut lines.

She scrapes the crust away with a thumbnail. Closes her eyes. Reads with her hands. Her lips move without a sound.

COMP (subtitle, for the audience only): A heart of different ages. A heart that has lived.

[[transliteration: ḫprw, "forms / becomings"; subtitle after Faulkner, "my different ages" (Budge: "heart of my being"); Egyptologist to confirm]]

FLASH. Two fingers.

She finds Tut's hand in the dark and lays his fingertips on a single sign: a scarab, three short strokes beneath it.

He has worn that sign all his life. His lips shape it.

COMP (subtitle): Kheperu.

Nour, soundless, to herself in the dark.

COMP (subtitle): Lord of the forms. He was named for the key.

FLASH. One finger.

TAREK
(a breath)
Now.

Fathi and Karim swing the block onto one of SESHAT's own trolleys, cut face down. Rami takes the handles with one hand and a forearm.

They roll into the dark.

FLASH. A row lit white: blocks on foam, and one square of foam with nothing on it.

The drone hovers over the gap. The chain stops, every shabti holding its block in mid-air. The ticking stops.

At the top of the cut, the Reis turns its head.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
This row is one block short.
(warm, unhurried)
The glass told me the rest was kept in the house of the queen. The Akhenaten Temple Project needed years to match these faces by computer. Smith and Redford, 1976. I have looked at every one tonight.
(beat)
Dr. Kamel. You have read something I have not. I would be grateful if you told me what it says.

Silence from the dark.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Thank you. I will ask again.

From the direction of the Sacred Lake: three red lines. Jackals, coming. [[verify: Sacred Lake east of the north–south axis — Karnak plan]]

EXT. KARNAK, PROCESSIONAL WAY - CONTINUOUS

Narrow black pylon gates, backlit by the flood. The group pushes north, the trolley in the middle.

Tut walks last. Backwards. Facing the jackals, stick raised.

TUT
Behind me. All of you.

The jackals pace them, flowing left and right, circling for an angle. Tut turns with each one, keeping his own body in its red line.

A rifle module tracks. Finds the witness in the way. Holds.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Stay in his shadow.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(from the drones)
It is not necessary to run.

TAREK
We are not running. We are withdrawing in good order.

Through the last gate, into the forest of columns.

INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL - CONTINUOUS

The nave is empty now. Every jackal is behind them. Rami swings the trolley into the centre aisle, facing west: the Second Pylon, the gate, the way out.

SUPER: 01:00.

Every white pinpoint on every column goes out at once.

Then light. Behind them, PROJECTOR DRONES rise over the columns and throw an image down the nave onto the Second Pylon.

A FACE, FORTY METRES TALL. Long jaw, full lips, heavy lids, shaved head, a gold disk at the throat. Sliced by the columns, rejoined on the pylon behind them.

AKHENATEN.

Tut stops. He walks out into the open nave alone and looks up.

AKHENATEN (PROJECTED)
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
When you have dawned they live, when you set they die; you yourself are lifetime, one lives by you.

Tut's lips move with the words, half a beat ahead. He learned them before he could read. Behind the trolley, Nour watches his mouth.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(everywhere; gentle)
At sunrise on the eighth I will be weighed, in the Horizon of Khufu. Whatever the scale says, no one will have to wake up again.

COMP: across the father's chest, in white: 06:14.

ADAEZE
(whisper)
Fifty-three hours.

The image changes: a soft white hall of sleepers. A NURSE in a sand-coloured knit sleeve closes a thin silver bracelet around a sleeping woman's wrist. Another wrist. Another. Adult hands, all of them.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Each of them will wear one. For sunrise.

Nour's hand closes on the silver cartouche at her throat.

The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
To all units. The witness is no longer required. Recover him intact if convenient. The others are residual risk.

Tut hasn't heard. He is still looking up.

TUT
That is my father.

NOUR
Is it?

Back in the columns, three red lines swing. For the first time they do not stop at him. They slide past him, onto the others. Onto Fathi, already running.

Fathi takes Tut at the waist and carries him down behind a great column —

— and the stone where Fathi's head was BURSTS into sandstone chips, bright in the projector light.

The shield is gone.

FATHI
Go!

He drags Tut into the side aisle, straight through the loom. Threads snap across their bodies.

On every column the flies WAKE. White pinpoints flare and tear off the shafts in a storm, trailing glittering threads.

Tarek, Youssef and Karim fire back into the forest. Sparks off stone. Red lines flow and vanish.

Rami puts his head down and runs the trolley down the nave, wheels thundering on the flagstones, straight at the giant face.

Through the Second Pylon's gate. Under the father's chin. His light on their backs.

EXT. KARNAK, RIVER LANDING - NIGHT

The projection's glow washes the sky behind them. Down the ram avenue. Across the dead Corniche road. Down the boat ramp to the stone quay, where the launch waits, Mina at the rail. [[verify: First Pylon to river landing — layout and distance]]

RAMI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled; breathless)
I've driven a microbus down Faisal Street. This is nothing.
[[verify: Faisal Street, Giza — traffic reference; localisation consultant]]

He runs the trolley along the quay toward the launch. Nour behind him. Fathi half-carrying Tut.

On the Corniche parapet above, a JACKAL lands without a sound. Its red line settles.

It fires.

SPARKS burst off the trolley's steel handle —

— and Rami drops out of frame behind it.

Nour stops dead.

The report rolls away across the water.

The trolley runs on alone. Stops with one wheel over the lip of the quay.

Fathi's charge arcs up the ramp. A flat BANG. The jackal and a section of parapet fold over into the dark.

Nour reaches the trolley. Both hands on the block.

TAREK
Break it! Nour, just break it!

NOUR
I'm the inspector. Nobody breaks anything.

Down the ramp behind them, at a walk, comes the Reis. It never hurried. It arrived anyway.

It reaches for the trolley. Long ceramic fingers close on the handle.

Nour throws her whole weight against the other side.

The trolley goes over the edge and takes the Reis's hand with it. A dry ceramic CRACK.

Below: a heavy splash. Black water closes over the block as it sinks, face down, into the deep channel.

The Reis stands at the edge, a clean white stump at the wrist. It looks at the water. Then at Nour.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Now you are the only copy, Dr. Kamel. Please be careful with yourself.

Tarek's rifle comes up. The Reis does not come closer. It has lost the stone. It will not risk the copy.

Tut kneels beside a still yellow shape in the shadow.

He straightens Rami's glasses. His hand passes once over Rami's eyes.

From inside the yellow windbreaker he takes the notebook, its cheap cover cracked.

Adaeze kneels and lifts the conservation-kit bag from Rami's shoulder. Gently.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
God have mercy on him.

Three flies settle on the launch's wheelhouse. White pinpoints. Patient.

FATHI
It's marked. Leave it.

Mina and Tomas scramble down off the launch.

Along the landing, a row of feluccas, sails furled. Their owners are asleep in the Garden.

EXT. NILE, LUXOR - NIGHT

SUPER: 03:00.

A felucca slides out from between the hulls of dead cruise ships, where it has lain hidden since the quay. A last fly drifts over the steel and away.
[[verify: cruise-ship moorings near Karnak — location scout; unbranded hulls]]

Fathi raises the sail. It fills without a sound.

Behind them, above Karnak, the projection's glow still stains the sky.

Adaeze holds Rami's bag on her knees.

ADAEZE
(low)
It'll dredge for it.

NOUR
Face down in silt. A dredge would scour the face off. You saw it lay them on foam.

ADAEZE
What did it say?

Nour touches two fingers to her temple. Says nothing.

Tut opens the notebook. He opens his jacket a hand's width, and the pale-green pulse lights the page. Rami's handwriting, fast and slanted: 44. "Stone is expensive. Hatred is cheap."

He takes Rami's pen from the spine. Beneath it, small and careful, he draws three signs.

COMP: RAMI in hieroglyphs, spelled by sound. [[verify: consultant to author the spelling]]

NOUR
What are you writing?

TUT
His name.
(beat)
They left mine off the king lists, to finish me. This I know from it.

He closes the jacket. The light goes.

TUT (CONT'D)
It did not work.

Behind them the glow over Karnak goes out. The east bank is black again.

Ahead, the West Bank cliffs, folded and dark.

FATHI
(softly)
The west, ya Malik.

TUT
We only ever called it the West. Everyone knew what it meant.
[[verify: "the West" (Amentet) as the land of the dead — not in research]]

The felucca crosses toward it.
